eight news now in nevada we certainly  know about  gun violence we had the highest number  of people killed in an  incident in modern history a nevada  democratic congresswoman  working there to pass gun control bills  house democrats say the  two gun control bills they are poised to  pass  would make the country safer our  washington correspondent alexandra limon  has the details of the measures  and also tells us why it's going to be  tough for them to actually become  law democrats are pushing for passage of  two bills to strengthen background  checks for gun sales  if you are a felon if you are a criminal  if you are deranged  well by god you shouldn't have access to  a weapon that's what this bill does  it has the support of about 90 percent  of americans  illinois congresswoman sherry bustos  says one bill would close the so-called  gun show loophole  by making it illegal for unlicensed  persons to transfer firearms to someone  else without a background check listen  i'm a father  of a five-year-old is in school i care  deeply about gun violence  republican congressman richard hudson  says these bills are not the solution  these bills again would have not stopped  a single  mass shooting but democrats say the  second bill which would extend the  background check period from 3 to 10  days  would give authorities more time to flag  people like the charleston church  shooter  nevada congresswoman dina titus says the  bills still respect the gun rights of  americans  but that doesn't mean that people who  shouldn't have guns can go out and use  them in our streets as weapons of war  republican senator marco rubio says he  doubts the bills will improve  gun violence the people aren't going to  follow the law are criminals  the people that are buying guns in the  back alley if they pass in the house the  bills would still need to gain support  from  every democrat and 10 republicans to  pass in the u.s senate  in washington alexandra limon
 

